"""Phase 4f -- second compression pass after Phase 5 left the paper at 22 pages.

Targets with slack left in the redundancy map; nothing protected is touched:
- detector and sampling-mechanism itemize lists -> inline prose (list spacing);
- 7.3 P2 re-quoted Appendix D.1 numbers (D.1 and tab:mechanism carry them);
- 2.2 relation-to-RUL paragraph and 6.9/6.10 tightened. 6.10 KEEPS its recommendation
  ('frequency-domain features admitted with care'): Section 7.4 no longer states it.
"""
from p4lib import Doc

d = Doc()
d.sub("The headline set comprises nine detectors, evaluated under one fixed hyperparameter protocol:\n\\begin{itemize}\n",
      "The headline set comprises nine detectors under one fixed hyperparameter protocol: an RMS-trend naive baseline, which alarms when the mean RMS exceeds $k\\sigma$ above its training baseline and is included to verify that the metric penalizes a weak detector; the $3\\sigma$ rule, alarming when the $\\ell_2$-norm health index exceeds three training standard deviations; an EWMA chart \\cite{roberts1959control} ($\\lambda=0.2$, $k=3.0$), sensitive to small sustained shifts; a deterministic two-sided tabular CUSUM chart \\cite{page1954continuous} ($k=0.5$, $h=5.0$) on the monitored magnitude, the classic change-point chart most sensitive to a small sustained mean shift such as slow degradation onset; Hotelling $T^2$ \\cite{hotelling1947multivariate} in a principal component analysis (PCA)-reduced subspace (rank $\\ll n$), so the covariance is well-conditioned; Isolation Forest \\cite{liu2008isolation}, an unsupervised ensemble that isolates anomalies with few random splits; and three deep reconstruction baselines---an LSTM autoencoder \\cite{malhotra2016lstm}, a temporal convolutional network autoencoder (dilated causal convolutions) and a lightweight reconstruction Transformer \\cite{vaswani2017attention}---each trained on normal feature-window sequences only and scoring a window by its reconstruction error.\n%ITEMS-START\n")
lines = d.s.split("\n")
a = lines.index("%ITEMS-START")
b = next(i for i in range(a, len(lines)) if lines[i].strip() == "\\end{itemize}")
removed = [ln for ln in lines[a + 1:b] if ln.strip()]
assert len(removed) == 7 and all(ln.lstrip().startswith("\\item") for ln in removed), removed
del lines[a:b + 1]
d.s = "\n".join(lines)
d.sub("under two mechanisms:\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item \\emph{aggregate:} take the mean within each bin of width $f$, exactly as a SCADA historian stores a bin-averaged value;\n\\item \\emph{decimate:} keep every $f$-th sample and discard the rest.\n\\end{itemize}\n",
      "under two mechanisms: \\emph{aggregate} takes the mean within each bin of width $f$, exactly as a SCADA historian stores a bin-averaged value, and \\emph{decimate} keeps every $f$-th sample and discards the rest.\n")
d.para("\\emph{Relation to published FEMTO/XJTU results.}",
       "\\emph{Relation to published FEMTO/XJTU results.} The IEEE PHM 2012 challenge and later remaining-useful-life (RUL) studies on FEMTO/\\PHMslashbreak PRONOSTIA and XJTU-SY \\cite{wang2020hybrid,nectoux2012pronostia} report a RUL-error score on deliberately truncated Test and Validation copies. It is not comparable to ours: they estimate a continuous RUL on runs with no observed failure and report no false-alarm rate, so their scores say nothing about whether an alarm would survive our $\\tau$ gate, whereas we measure early-warning lead time on complete run-to-failure trajectories; tabulating one against the other would be a category error. The classic normalized-RMS health indicator with a first-prediction-time threshold, the baseline underlying most of that literature, is our rms\\_trend detector; re-evaluating specific published architectures under the gated metric (which requires their original code) is future work.")
d.para("On IMS, time-domain amplitude features (rms\\_only or time\\_only)",
       "On IMS, time-domain amplitude features (rms\\_only or time\\_only) give each of the six non-sequence detectors its highest or tied-highest validity, and adding spectral, envelope or all features never improves validity for any of them (Table~\\ref{tab:ablation_general}). For the SPC charts the effect is sharp: rms\\_only is the unique group at which $3\\sigma$ and EWMA reach full validity (1.00 at 0\\% pre-onset FAR), while spectral, envelope and all collapse all four charts to 0.33 by driving their pre-onset FAR to 42--63\\%, the variance-sensitive charts reacting violently to noisy extra dimensions. Deep SVDD fails the opposite way: its pre-onset FAR stays near 0\\% under every feature set, yet spectral features cut its validity to 0.33 (0.00 for spectral\\_only) by delaying or suppressing the alarm---missed detections, not false alarms. Under a false-alarm budget every detector should therefore be restricted to time-domain amplitude features, and frequency-domain features admitted with care, most of all for the SPC charts. Raw lead inverts this ranking without overturning it: on Isolation Forest the envelope group gives the longest mean lead (99.4~h) and rms\\_only the shortest (32.0~h), but the long-lead groups accrue 23--29\\% pre-onset FAR against 0.2\\% for rms\\_only, buying their lead by firing indiscriminately; per run, spectral features flip $3\\sigma$, EWMA and Hotelling $T^2$ from valid to invalid on test~2 at 100\\% pre-onset FAR while Isolation Forest stays valid in both settings. Repeated at every coarsening factor, rms\\_only stays uniquely best at native and $2\\times$ resolution ($f \\le 2$), matching the argument that bin-averaging preserves the amplitude statistic; at $f \\ge 5$ only a handful of windows survive per run, valid fractions move in steps of 1/3, and whether the advantage holds at the coarsest SCADA rates is not resolvable at $n=3$.")
d.para("The prediction that follows is about noise, not about detectors:",
       "The prediction that follows is about noise, not detectors: the advantage of aggregation should grow as the noise floor rises. It does, monotonically, from $-0.35$~h at the native noise level to $+6.60$~h at 10~dB, and an ordinary moving-average or Kalman filter on the decimated stream recovers it (Appendix~D.1, Table~\\ref{tab:mechanism}).")
d.para("Sweeping the alarm-threshold percentile traces",
       "Sweeping the alarm-threshold percentile traces a prognostics analog of the ROC curve (Figure~\\ref{fig:tradeoffs}, Table~\\ref{tab:tradeoff}): tightening the threshold lowers pre-onset FAR and lead together, so an operator can select an operating point for a stated false-alarm budget. On the achievable frontier Hotelling $T^2$ and Isolation Forest dominate the upper left, while the naive RMS-trend baseline collapses to near-zero lead once the threshold tightens (1.3~h at the 99.5th percentile). Hotelling $T^2$ has the highest raw lead of any control chart but exceeds the $\\tau=10\\%$ budget at every threshold tested, so it has no valid operating point on IMS; EWMA and CUSUM behave the same way, their pre-onset FAR sitting at 19.0--22.1\\% across the swept thresholds, so neither memory-based chart should be recommended under a 10\\% budget. Deep SVDD occupies the opposite corner, within budget ($\\le 0.6\\%$ FAR) only by firing late ($\\le 14.8$~h of lead). $3\\sigma$ reaches a valid point, 56.3~h of lead at 8.9\\% FAR at the 99.5th percentile, and is the recommended chart under a 10\\% budget, a ranking that the raw-lead column of Table~\\ref{tab:imsdet}, led by the deep models, would by itself obscure. The ten XJTU bearings (Figure~\\ref{fig:tradeoffs}b) give the same qualitative shape at shorter absolute leads, so the operating-point selection generalizes beyond the laboratory rig.")
d.save()
